POINT 5 -- no deadlock from calling s.atomicWrite under s.mu. MET; checked
myself rather than taken on trust.
  $ awk '/^func \(s \*Storage\) atomicWrite/,/^}/' internal/services/storage/storage.go | grep -nE '\bs\.[A-Za-z]+|Lock\(|Unlock\('
    (no output)
  atomicWrite's body calls only filepath.Dir/Base, os.MkdirAll, os.CreateTemp,
  f.Write, f.Close, os.Chmod, os.Rename, os.Remove -- no method on *Storage and
  no lock at all, so its reachable set contains no lock and cannot close a
  cycle. The holders:
  $ grep -n "func (s \*Storage) EnableEncryptionWithProvider" -A3 migration.go -> s.mu.Lock(); defer s.mu.Unlock()  (lines 24-26)
  $ same for DisableEncryption (lines 115-117)
  I also enumerated every acquisition site in the package
  ($ awk '/^func /{fn=$0} /\.(Lock|RLock)\(\)/{...}' internal/services/storage/*.go)
  to check the second-order risk, since the helpers do take cacheMu via
  invalidateCache while holding s.mu. Ordering is uniformly s.mu -> cacheMu:
  Storage.Lock() takes s.mu then cacheMu; ReadFileContext takes cacheMu and
  RUnlocks it *before* s.mu.RLock() (storage.go, "s.cacheMu.RUnlock()" precedes
  "s.mu.RLock()"). No site takes s.mu while holding cacheMu, so no reverse edge
  exists either.
  Live corroboration: the -race package suite exercises EnableEncryption/
  DisableEncryption -> atomicWrite under s.mu and completed in 307s against a
  600s timeout without hanging.

