THE ARM'S DISCLOSURE ABOUT TestMigrationRoundTripLeavesNoStagingFiles --
DISPUTED. The claim ("not independently mutation-discriminating; no portable
mutation of this diff makes it fail alone") is falsified.
  MUTATION E1 (scratch, confined to migration.go): in encryptFileWithRecipient,
  replaced the atomicWrite call with hand-rolled staging under the correct
  StagingSuffix convention that publishes the bytes but only removes the
  staging file on error paths -- i.e. exactly the "forgot the cleanup" slip,
  and the same hand-rolled shape wt-alt actually wrote.
    $ go test -count=1 ./internal/services/storage/
      --- FAIL: TestMigrationRoundTripLeavesNoStagingFiles (5.72s)
      FAIL
    That is the whole failure list for the package: the test failed alone.
  So the test is stronger than advertised. It discriminates the leaked-staging-
  file class, which is precisely the regression a future refactor away from
  atomicWrite would introduce.
  For contrast, two mutations it does NOT catch, so the boundary is on record:
    E2 (atomicWrite leaks on every write): caught, but not alone --
      TestReadFileConcurrentWriterNeverServesStale and
      TestRollbackEncryptionPublishesByRenameNotWrite fail too.
    E1b (staging file created above the `isAgeEncrypted` early return, so only
      the already-encrypted skip path leaks): NOT caught -- full package suite
      printed "ok budget2/internal/services/storage 50.465s". No test in the
      package drives a migration helper over an already-encrypted file with a
      leftover check. A real but narrow blind spot; not a criterion here.

ROLLBACK'S SIGNATURE STILL ONLY LOGS -- opinion, as asked, on work that is out
of scope for this task. Acceptable as shipped, and now materially less urgent
than it was before this change. The reason: pre-fix, the most likely cause of a
swallowed rollback failure was a non-writable destination, which M1 shows was
both reachable and silent. Publishing by rename removes that cause; what remains
reachable is directory-level failure (ENOSPC, read-only mount), under which the
encrypt that triggered the rollback would almost certainly have failed the same
way. The residual defect is real -- EnableEncryption's "failed to encrypt <file>"
cannot distinguish "restored" from "left partially encrypted", and a caller that
wanted to warn the user which one happened has no way to -- but it is an
error-reporting gap on a path that no longer has a common trigger, not a data
integrity gap. Worth a separate task alongside F3 (DisableEncryption's missing
rollback), not a reason to reject F2.

